section_notes={
'FOUNDATIONS-SPIKES':('The literal terms spike, firing rate, synapse, excitatory/inhibitory input and recurrent connectivity identify different neural objects and actions; removing them would break the later feedback cycle.','The tuning slope and its units support the information derivative. Population index versus physical position prevents a later map/neuronal-axis confusion. Rate map, CA3/CA1 connectivity and grid fields prepare the spatial chapters. Retain the negative-rate limit and model-versus-measurement distinction.'),
'FOUNDATIONS-POISSON':('Poisson process, CV and Fano factor are established names for different variability laws or measures, each followed by its actual meaning; they must not become interchangeable plain synonyms.','The silence example shows why zero counts do not prove zero rate; pooling shows why independent variance behaves differently from shared noise. Keep conditional independence and full-train qualifications. Only the unused factorial-moments label is removable, not the proof.'),
'FOUNDATIONS-COVARIANCE':('Covariance and correlation are necessary exact metric names; their equations and units distinguish them.','The variance-of-sums equation later supports both readout and common-source inference. Nonzero variance, finite moments and lack of an independence converse are genuine conditions. The MSD split prepares the bias calculation; deletion would hide mean motion within noise.'),
'FOUNDATIONS-ATTRACTOR':('State, fixed point, continuous attractor and attractor manifold refer to successively different dynamical objects; neutral is explained directly.','The two perturbations are required because one recovers while the other changes memory with coherent rates. Their initial rates, coordinate map, positive-rate domain and dynamics make the outcome calculable. Arrow distinction and nonperpendicular-projection warning prepare the later left vector.'),
'FOUNDATIONS-DIFFUSION':('Brownian motion and root mean squared displacement name the process and its displacement scale, not vague synonyms for noise.','Increment assumptions justify variance addition; the continuous-path definition precedes use. The numeric example compares square-root spreading with linear bias and a nontrivial crossover. The covariance proof supplies the later averaging integral. Keep boundary, dimensional and equal-coordinate limits.'),
'FOUNDATIONS-RANDOMNESS':('Fixed expected-rate map and wandering represented value describe different axes of randomness; no new label is needed.','The explicit revisit statement and possibility of both variations prevent treating place-field irregularity as a drift measurement. The map then compares the three questions with the same distinctions, and the brief final limit prevents a false single-circuit conclusion.'),
'MEMORY-SPIKES':('Synaptic activation and spike count are distinct state objects; neural transfer function, weak fluctuation approximation and preferred angle are taught through their roles.','Unit jumps, exponential decay, input sum, compensated noise moments and Gaussian rewrite each add a needed step. Large-population scaling explains what makes input fluctuations small. Keep conditional noise independence, units, finite-network limit and tuned-family condition. The displayed trace distinguishes spike events from accumulated activation.'),
'MEMORY-LINEARIZATION':('Linearization and left/right zero eigenvector are necessary exact names; the text defines their equations and different uses before projection.','Taylor cancellation, tangent differentiation, normalization and stable-mode cancellation supply separate proof steps. The tilted example earns its length by disproving a perpendicular-projection shortcut with an exact endpoint. Keep its caption and concluding shift; remove only the duplicate prefigure announcement.'),
'MEMORY-DIFFUSION':('Diffusion tensor is necessary for the multi-coordinate covariance object.','The multiplication by v, independent-increment contraction and factor2 match give the result rather than quoting it. Per-cell interpretation needs both rate variability and projection sensitivity. Time-window and weak-noise conditions must stay; tensor off-diagonal explanation prevents confusing independent spikes with independent coordinate motion.'),
'MEMORY-SYMMETRIC':('Use the already taught neural transfer function for the input-to-rate mapping. Symmetric weights is a precise algebraic condition, not a claim that the linearized matrix is symmetric.','Derivative identities, transpose step, normalization and substitution are all needed for the tuning formula. The source numerator discrepancy must remain because a reader checking main2 would otherwise see a conflict. Mutual inhibition exposes constant diffusion despite changing rates; keep interior-domain and coordinate-scale warnings.'),
'MEMORY-LONG-TIME':('Ito convention, Fokker Planck equation and Dirac delta are necessary exact terms and are explained through evaluation, density evolution and point-mass roles.','The wrapped sum avoids an unused Jacobi-function prerequisite. Its source link remains after removing writing commentary. Moment integration and the linear-D example answer why a state-varying local coefficient need not curve the variance graph. Keep source-factor correction, but remove the repeated sentence explaining the writer choice.'),
'MEMORY-DRIFT-SCOPE':('Systematic drift and Stratonovich convention are required because the chapter separates mean motion from variance and compares noise evaluation rules.','The conversion term is short and exact; deleting it would leave an unsupported D-prime/2 assertion. Keep the small-noise scaling, decoder/domain conditions and joint-likelihood warning. They stop a leading variance result being read as a general drift or correlated-noise theorem.'),
'MEMORY-FIGURES':('Activity bump is explained as a peak across preferred angles, avoiding confusion with a peak over physical position.','Panel-by-panel claims tell the reader what is tested and how rate traces, spike marks, theoretical diffusion and lower bound differ. The distinction between model simulation and animal evidence is necessary. The ending moves from dynamical D to the need for a statistical bound.'),
'MEMORY-FI':('Likelihood, score, Fisher information, information rate, unbiased, Cramer Rao bound and prior distribution are technical words used by later calculations; each meaning is supplied where needed.','The log product, zero-mean score and Poisson cancellation are proof steps. The rate-peak/baseline figure makes two informative distinctions that the formula alone can hide. Keep local-stationarity, positive-rate, regularity, prior and attainability limits. Those are not ornamental caveats.'),
'MEMORY-BOUND':('The mathematical words proportional, converse, counterexample and positive-semidefinite are appropriate for the recorded mathematics baseline; the claims they distinguish are explicitly shown.','The Cauchy Schwarz vectors make the proof short and complete. Pointwise equality, exponential sufficient class and linear-family counterexample must all stay to qualify the source. The balanced and unbalanced two-cell states show why a lower bound need not be attained. The matrix residual proves the coupled-direction result. Remove only the duplicate figure announcement.'),
'MEMORY-COMPARISONS':('Peak rate, tuning width, preferred position and coordinate directions refer to already defined quantities; the repaired radial-shape restriction explains symmetry without adding isotropic jargon.','The separate N, firing-scale and tau comparisons are needed because different held quantities change conclusions. The integral exposes the active-volume and squared-slope factors; the firing-budget alternative demonstrates a changed result. Keep boundaries, coverage, correlations and sensory/internal tuning condition.'),
'MEMORY-READOUT-SETUP':('Translation invariance is defined for readout error statistics, and the Gaussian equivalent observation is described as information from spikes rather than an extra physical sensor.','The cue versus initial value distinction, covariance proof and common readout variance supply different assumptions needed for error addition. The timeline restates the two errors visually, and the short interpretation prevents confusing the drawn estimate with the path endpoint. Keep prior/boundary and approximation limits.'),
'MEMORY-NAIVE':('Naive labels the specified equal-weight estimator, not an insult or a generic bad observer.','The Brownian covariance reminder is needed at the integral. The two triangles provide the missing cross-time calculation, and optimization balances terms that change in opposite directions. The final conclusion gives the consequence of equal weighting rather than only a number.'),
'MEMORY-IDEAL':('Precision, hyperbolic tangent, inverse hyperbolic tangent and cotangent are required by the backward recursion and solution; their equations are short definitions rather than unnecessary vocabulary.','Independent precision addition, backward variance growth and new-bin addition are different steps. The initial condition and separated integral justify the closed form. Keep finite-versus-asymptotic improvement, characteristic time, variance/SD distinction and source-method qualification. Removing those would exaggerate saturation.'),
'MEMORY-PARTIAL':('Independent spike retention is a specific sampling rule; information fraction cannot be replaced by an arbitrary fraction of neurons.','The alpha scaling changes observation time and uncertainty while D stays fixed. The numbers isolate readout cost from prior storage loss. Keep the selected-subset condition and two-second common storage contribution because these prevent the wrong intervention interpretation.'),
'MEMORY-EXTENSIONS':('Rate relaxation and Gaussian noise are precise changes of model, introduced before the block matrix or new likelihood.','Two-block tangent/left-vector algebra is necessary to justify tau+tau_m. Noise placement and symmetry restrict the result. Constant q is retained in both information and projection, showing why equality conditions depend on noise law. The reconstruction/source-normalization note matters because source equations omit visible factors and should not silently determine the guide convention.'),
'MEMORY-FIG3-AND-CAUTION':('The normalized curve formulas reuse taught functions; no new specialist vocabulary is required.','Panel interpretation, storage exclusion and partial-access shifts each add a distinct reading fact. Remove only the empty prefigure sentence, keeping its interpretation afterward. The printed inequality check and strict example are necessary because blindly copying main6 would reverse an inequality; author-correction and local-Gaussian limits must remain. The final integrator paragraph distinguishes variance from leak.')
}

